## t0-reverse-lines
Read all lines into list, then print in reverse. Use list append.

```tin
troufu lines = [];
troufu line = bounfou();
tafashou line ~= furfifan
    marboun(lines, line);
    line = bounfou();
trushou
troufu i = utoufa(lines) - 1;
tafashou i >= 0
    binmi lines[i];
    i = i - 1;
trushou
```

## t1-digits
Sum of digits and reversed digits as integer, keeping sign (unless 0). Input |n| ≤ 10^15, fits in 64-bit integer fine (10^15 < 9.2e18). Wait, shufa parses decimal digits to integer — 10^15 is fine as 64-bit. So we can just use integer arithmetic! Sum digits via repeated % 10, / 10. Reversed: build integer.

For negative, handle sign: work with abs, then negate.

Edge: n=0 → "0 0". Also reversed with trailing zeros dropped naturally since we build integer. E.g. -4500: abs 4500, digits sum 9, reversed 54 → -54. Good.

```tin
troufu line = bounfou();
tafashou line ~= furfifan
    troufu n = shufa(line);
    troufu sign = 1;
    sitrartu n < 0 fisu drufirsa
        sign = -1;
        n = -n;
    drumin
    troufu sum = 0;
    troufu rev = 0;
    sitrartu n == 0 fisu drufirsa
        sum = 0;
        rev = 0;
    drumin
    tafashou n > 0
        troufu d = n % 10;
        sum = sum + d;
        rev = rev * 10 + d;
        n = n / 10;
    trushou
    sitrartu sign == -1 dra rev ~= 0 fisu drufirsa
        rev = -rev;
    drumin
    binmi sum + " " + rev;
    line = bounfou();
trushou
```

Wait: `n / 10` for positive n rounds toward zero — fine, positive. Good.

But careful: what if input n is 0 — sum 0 rev 0, print "0 0". Good. Negative with rev 0? Can't happen except n=0. sign == -1 and rev==0 would mean n=0 with negative sign — impossible. Fine, but I added condition anyway.

## t1-floordiv
Need floor division and floor-mod. Tinbousan's `/` rounds toward zero. For floor: if (a % b != 0) and ((a < 0) != (b < 0)), then q = a/b - 1 else q = a/b. Or use formula. Let me write:

```tin
tumumin floordiv(a, b)
    troufu q = a / b;
    sitrartu a % b ~= 0 dra ((a < 0) butrouta (b < 0)) dra shutoun ((a < 0) dra (b < 0)) fisu drufirsa
        q = q - 1;
    drumin
    drur q;
drumin tumumin
```

Hmm, condition: a%b != 0 and signs differ. Signs differ: (a<0) != (b<0). In Tinbousan booleans: `((a < 0) dra shutoun (b < 0)) butrouta (shutoun (a < 0) dra (b < 0))`. That's XOR. Let me define xor as: signs differ = `(a < 0 dra b >= 0) butrouta (a >= 0 dra b < 0)`. Simpler:

`((a < 0) dra (b > 0)) butrouta ((a > 0) dra (b < 0))` — signs differ. Note b can't be 0, a can be... a could be 0 but then a%b==0 so condition false anyway.

So:

```tin
sitrartu a % b ~= 0 dra (((a < 0) dra (b > 0)) butrouta ((a > 0) dra (b < 0))) fisu drufirsa
    q = q - 1;
drumin
```

Then r = a - b*q.

Parse two integers separated by one space. Need to split the line. I'll write split function.

```tin
tumumin splitsp(s)
    troufu parts = [];
    troufu cur = "";
    troufu i = 0;
    tafashou i < utoufa(s)
        troufu c = s[i];
        sitrartu c == " " fisu drufirsa
            marboun(parts, cur);
            cur = "";
        drumin tru drufirsa
            cur = cur + c;
        drumin
        i = i + 1;
    trushou
    marboun(parts, cur);
    drur parts;
drumin tumumin
```

Careful: variable redeclaration inside loop — "Declaring an existing variable again simply assigns to it." Inside the while body each pass re-executes `troufu c = s[i]` which re-assigns. That's fine per reference: "Declaring an existing variable again simply assigns to it." And visibility is from declaration point to end of function. Fine.

Then:

```tin
troufu line = bounfou();
tafashou line ~= furfifan
    troufu parts = splitsp(line);
    troufu a = shufa(parts[0]);
    troufu b = shufa(parts[1]);
    troufu q = a / b;
    sitrartu a % b ~= 0 dra (((a < 0) dra (b > 0)) butrouta ((a > 0) dra (b < 0))) fisu drufirsa
        q = q - 1;
    drumin
    troufu r = a - b * q;
    binmi q + " " + r;
    line = bounfou();
trushou
```

Wait — negative modulo in Tinbousan: `-7 % 2`: -7 - 2*(-7/2) = -7 - 2*(-3) = -7 + 6 = -1. Good, sign follows dividend (truncated). For `7 -2`: 7 % -2 = 7 - (-2)*(7/-2) = 7 - (-2)*(-3) = 7 - 6 = 1. q trunc = -3, but floor should be -4, r = 7 - (-2)(-4) = 7 - 8 = -1. Signs differ (a>0, b<0), a%b=1 ≠ 0, so q=-4. Good.

## t1-palindromes
Keep only letters and digits, ignore case. Build normalized string, compare with reverse.

```tin
tumumin isalnum(c)
    drur (c >= "a" dra c <= "z") butrouta (c >= "A" dra c <= "Z") butrouta (c >= "0" dra c <= "9");
drumin tumumin

tumumin lower(c)
    troufu ups = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ";
    troufu los = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz";
    troufu i = 0;
    troufu r = c;
    tafashou i < 26
        sitrartu c == ups[i] fisu drufirsa
            r = los[i];
        drumin
        i = i + 1;
    trushou
    drur r;
drumin tumumin
```

Main:

```tin
troufu line = bounfou();
tafashou line ~= furfifan
    troufu norm = "";
    troufu i = 0;
    tafashou i < utoufa(line)
        troufu c = line[i];
        sitrartu isalnum(c) fisu drufirsa
            norm = norm + lower(c);
        drumin
        i = i + 1;
    trushou
    troufu ok = sibitrou;
    troufu j = 0;
    troufu L = utoufa(norm);
    tafashou j < L / 2 + 1 dra ok fisu... 
```

Hmm wait, condition must be boolean: `j < (L + 1) / 2 dra ok`. Actually `j <= L / 2` works for integer L: for L even, j < L/2; for L odd, j < L/2+1, i.e., j <= L/2. So condition `j <= L / 2 dra ok` — but precedence: comparisons bind tighter than dra, so `(j <= L/2) dra ok`. Good.

```tin
    troufu ok = sibitrou;
    troufu j = 0;
    troufu L = utoufa(norm);
    tafashou j <= L / 2 dra ok
        sitrartu norm[j] ~= norm[L - 1 - j] fisu drufirsa
            ok = fufir;
        drumin
        j = j + 1;
    trushou
    sitrartu ok fisu drufirsa
        binmi "yes";
    drumin tru drufirsa
        binmi "no";
    drumin
    line = bounfou();
trushou
```

Note `lower` scans 26 each char — for lines up to maybe thousands of chars this is fine (step limit 50M).

Empty norm: L=0, loop doesn't run, ok true → yes. Good.

## t1-rank
First line: integers in non-decreasing order, separated by single spaces, at most 1000. Following lines: queries (one integer each). For each query print count of integers < query, space, found/missing.

Parse first line into list of ints. Then for each query, count smaller = binary search or linear scan (1000 elements, fine). found = check membership: since sorted, count of < q, and check if any equals q — e.g., count smaller than q+1... but q+1 could overflow? |q| ≤ 10^6 per... actually queries could be any integer; problem says "Each following line holds one integer, a query." Doesn't bound query value explicitly. But values at most 10^6? Not stated for queries. Let's be safe: linear scan once computing both: count smaller, and found flag.

```tin
tumumin splitsp(s) ... (as before)

troufu first = bounfou();
troufu toks = splitsp(first);
troufu xs = [];
troufu ti = 0;
tafashou ti < utoufa(toks)
    marboun(xs, shufa(toks[ti]));
    ti = ti + 1;
trushou
troufu line = bounfou();
tafashou line ~= furfifan
    troufu q = shufa(line);
    troufu smaller = 0;
    troufu found = fufir;
    troufu i = 0;
    tafashou i < utoufa(xs)
        sitrartu xs[i] < q fisu drufirsa
            smaller = smaller + 1;
        drumin
        sitrartu xs[i] == q fisu drufirsa
            found = sibitrou;
        drumin
        i = i + 1;
    trushou
    sitrartu found fisu drufirsa
        binmi smaller + " found";
    drumin tru drufirsa
        binmi smaller + " missing";
    drumin
    line = bounfou();
trushou
```

Edge: first line could be empty ("zero or more integers"). Then splitsp("") returns [""], shufa("") errors! Need to handle empty first line: if line is "", xs = []. Check: `sitrartu first == "" fisu drufirsa xs stays empty`. Let me guard:

```tin
troufu xs = [];
sitrartu first ~= "" fisu drufirsa
    ... parse ...
drumin
```

Actually if the first line is empty string, utir... simpler:

```tin
sitrartu first == "" fisu drufirsa
    // nothing
drumin tru drufirsa
    parse and fill xs
drumin
```

But `//` comment; empty branch is fine (no statements). But is an empty branch allowed? "The statements of the first true one run" — zero statements should be fine. Alternatively use a different structure. I'll write:

```tin
sitrartu first ~= "" fisu drufirsa
    troufu toks = splitsp(first);
    ...
drumin
```

If first == "", skip. Variables declared inside a branch remain visible to end of function — toks, ti declared inside the branch are visible after? "A variable declared anywhere in a function, including inside a loop or a branch, is visible from that point to the end of the function." Declaring inside a conditional that doesn't execute means the variable is uninitialized? Hmm, risky. Since xs is declared outside and I only mutate it inside the branch, that's safe. toks/ti declared inside the branch and not used after — fine either way, but to be safe I'll declare them outside.

Actually, even simpler and robust: make splitsp robust... no, shufa("") is an error regardless. Let me just guard.

Also, queries: values might be out of 64-bit? "one integer, a query" — assume within 64-bit as printable input. Fine.

## t1-rotate
First line k (|k| ≤ 10^9). Then zero or more lines of items. Rotate right by k; negative k rotates left. Print items one per line, each exactly as read.

Compute effective shift: m = number of items. If m == 0 print nothing. k mod m: right rotation by k means item at index i moves to (i + k) mod m. Output[j] = input[(j - k) mod m]. Let me just compute r = k mod m (handle negative). Easier: rotate right by k ≡ rotate left by (-k). Let me compute shift = k mod m in [0, m-1]. Since Tinbousan % truncates, for negative k, k % m gives negative or zero. Adjust: 

r = k % m; if r < 0 then r = r + m. Then output for j in 0..m-1: items[(j - r + m) % m]. Hmm (j - r) could be negative. Compute idx = (j - r) % m; if idx < 0 idx += m. Then print items[idx].

Simpler approach: newlist[i] = items[(i - r mod m + m) mod m]. Let me just do: for output position p from 0 to m-1, print items[(p + m - r) % m]. Since p + m - r ≥ 0 (as r ≤ m-1 and p ≥ 0 → p + m - r ≥ m - r ≥ 1 > 0... wait if r = m-1, p=0: m - (m-1) = 1 ≥ 0. Good, always non-negative). So idx = (p + m - r) % m works without adjustment.

Check example: items [a,b,c], k=1 → r=1. p=0: idx=(0+3-1)%3=2 → c. p=1: 0 → a. p=2: 1 → b. Output c a b. Rotating right by 1 moves last to front: c,a,b. Correct.

Negative k, e.g. k=-1, m=3: r = -1 % 3 = -1 (truncated), then r<0 → r=2. p=0: (0+3-2)%3=1 → b. Output b c a. Rotating left by 1: b,c,a. Correct.

```tin
troufu ks = bounfou();
troufu k = shufa(ks);
troufu items = [];
troufu line = bounfou();
tafashou line ~= furfifan
    marboun(items, line);
    line = bounfou();
trushou
troufu m = utoufa(items);
sitrartu m > 0 fisu drufirsa
    troufu r = k % m;
    sitrartu r < 0 fisu drufirsa
        r = r + m;
    drumin
    sutrarsu p = 0 sou m - 1
        binmi items[(p + m - r) % m];
    moun p
drumin
```

Note `moun p` — name after moun must be the loop variable, p. Good.

Hmm, `(p + m - r) % m` — all non-negative, fine.

## t1-weekday
Gregorian calendar, dates 0001-01-01 to 9999-12-31. Compute day of week. Use a known algorithm. 

Days from civil (Howard Hinnant's algorithm) or use Sakamoto's method. Let me use Sakamoto:

```c
int dow(int y, int m, int d) {
    static int t[] = {0, 3, 2, 5, 0, 3, 5, 1, 4, 6, 2, 4};
    if (m < 3) y -= 1;
    return (y + y/4 - y/100 + y/400 + t[m-1] + d) % 7;
}
```
Result: 0 = Sunday.

Verify with 2000-01-01 → Saturday. m=1 <3 → y=1999. y/4=499, y/100=19, y/400=4. t[0]=0. Sum = 1999+499-19+4+0+1 = 2484. 2484 % 7 = 2484 - 7*354=2478 → 6. 6=Saturday. Correct.

But careful with negative intermediates in Tinbousan: y + y/4 - y/100 + y/400 is positive for y ≥ 0 (year 1 min → y could be 0 after decrement: year 1, m<3 → y=0; sum = 0+0-0+0+t+d ≥ 0; fine, and % with positive divisor gives non-negative since dividend non-negative). Actually if m>=3, y ≥ 1. If m < 3 and year 1, y = 0, sum = t[m-1] + d > 0. So sum ≥ 0 always. Good, `% 7` gives 0..6, no adjustment needed.

Map: 0 Sunday, 1 Monday, ..., 6 Saturday.

Parsing date "YYYY-MM-DD": year = chars 0-3, month 5-6, day 8-9. Use shufa on substrings. Need substring function.

```tin
tumumin substr(s, a, b)   // chars from index a to b inclusive
    troufu r = "";
    troufu i = a;
    tafashou i <= b
        r = r + s[i];
        i = i + 1;
    trushou
    drur r;
drumin tumumin
```

Then y = shufa(substr(line,0,3)), m = shufa(substr(line,5,6)), d = shufa(substr(line,8,9)).

t[] as list: troufu t = [0,3,2,5,0,3,5,1,4,6,2,4]; t[m-1].

```tin
troufu line = bounfou();
tafashou line ~= furfifan
    troufu y = shufa(substr(line, 0, 3));
    troufu m = shufa(substr(line, 5, 6));
    troufu d = shufa(substr(line, 8, 9));
    sitrartu m < 3 fisu drufirsa
        y = y - 1;
    drumin
    troufu t = [0, 3, 2, 5, 0, 3, 5, 1, 4, 6, 2, 4];
    troufu w = (y + y / 4 - y / 100 + y / 400 + t[m - 1] + d) % 7;
    utir (w) {
    sourmar 0:
        binmi "Sunday";
    sourmar 1:
        binmi "Monday";
    sourmar 2:
        binmi "Tuesday";
    sourmar 3:
        binmi "Wednesday";
    sourmar 4:
        binmi "Thursday";
    sourmar 5:
        binmi "Friday";
    sourmar 6:
        binmi "Saturday";
    }
    line = bounfou();
trushou
```

Note: declaring t inside the loop each pass re-assigns — fine.

Also careful: integer division `y/4` positive. Good.

## t2-bignum
Add and multiply big numbers up to 100 digits. Must implement arbitrary precision with lists of digits. Since inputs are decimal strings, I'll do digit-array arithmetic.

Plan: represent number as list of digits, least significant first. Parse string to list of int digits reversed.

```tin
tumumin parsebig(s)
    troufu ds = [];
    troufu i = utoufa(s) - 1;
    tafashou i >= 0
        marboun(ds, shufa(s[i]));
        i = i - 1;
    trushou
    drur ds;
drumin tumumin
```

shufa on a one-char digit string returns the int. Good.

Add:

```tin
tumumin addbig(a, b)
    troufu r = [];
    troufu carry = 0;
    troufu i = 0;
    tafashou i < utoufa(a) butrouta i < utoufa(b) butrouta carry ~= 0
        troufu s = carry;
        sitrartu i < utoufa(a) fisu drufirsa
            s = s + a[i];
        drumin
        sitrartu i < utoufa(b) fisu drufirsa
            s = s + b[i];
        drumin
        marboun(r, s % 10);
        carry = s / 10;
        i = i + 1;
    trushou
    drur r;
drumin tumumin
```

Condition must be boolean: `(i < utoufa(a)) butrouta (i < utoufa(b)) butrouta (carry ~= 0)`. Good.

